Both are fixed and verified.

## What was wrong

Not a config or permissions problem — a **half-deleted install**. In `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`, pip's `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory was still on disk, but the actual `pip/` package directory it describes was gone. So:

- `pip`/`pip3` existed at `/usr/local/bin` as scripts that begin `from pip._internal.cli.main import main`
- that import failed with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`
- and `python3 -m pip` failed the same way, since the module genuinely wasn't installed

The leftover metadata also made the obvious repair fail: `python3 -m ensurepip` reported *"Requirement already satisfied: pip … (24.3.1)"* and exited 0 without reinstalling anything, because it only looked at `dist-info` and never tried the import.

## What I did

1. Backed up and removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` so ensurepip would stop being satisfied by it.
2. Ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip`, which reinstalled pip 24.3.1 from Python's own bundled wheel (no network needed).
3. Found a second interpreter, `/usr/bin/python3` (Debian 3.11), which had never had pip and has no `ensurepip`; installed it through the packaging system with `apt-get install python3-pip` (23.0.1), rather than forcing a system install against PEP 668.
4. Cleaned up the backup and test artifacts.

## Check

`python3 -m pip --version`, `pip --version` and `pip3 --version` all report 24.3.1 (3.13); `/usr/bin/python3 -m pip --version` reports 23.0.1 (3.11). A real `pip install --target` of `six` completed and imported, and a dry-run resolution now exits 0.

Two things worth knowing:

- `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` is present, so **package installs into the 3.11 distro Python will still refuse** with an "externally-managed-environment" error. That's PEP 668 working as intended, not the bug you hit — use `--user`/a venv there, or `apt` for system packages. The 3.13 install has no such marker.
- Your `PATH` puts `/usr/local/bin` ahead of `/usr/bin`, so bare `pip` is the 3.13 one. If you were previously seeing behaviour from the 3.11 one, that's the difference.

Nothing was written to the repository — the fault was in the system Python, and I left the repo untouched.

